<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Academic Achievement and Skill Development – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve conflicts in group settings. Text: Summarize complex texts for peers.
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
            <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can resolve simple conflicts in group study sessions by suggesting fair solutions and using clarifying language (e.g., says, "Let's split the tasks evenly so everyone has a fair part. I'll take notes, and you can make the slides.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can summarize the main points of an academic article for peers, highlighting key advice and strategies (e.g., "This article explains that setting study goals improves focus. Juan pointed out that reviewing notes daily helps retain information better.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can mediate disagreements in group projects by rephrasing ideas and ensuring everyone's opinions are heard (e.g., "Mariana suggested that if we combine both ideas, we can create a stronger project. What does everyone think?").</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students discuss in pairs common study challenges they or their friends face (e.g., "procrastination," "time management," "lack of focus"). Teacher asks a few pairs to share. Then, the teacher presents a simple scenario of a group study conflict (e.g., "Two students have different ideas for how to complete a project task, and they can't agree. One wants to focus on research, the other on design."). Students briefly discuss how they would resolve it, practicing inclusive language and suggesting solutions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for mediating and summarizing, such as: "It sounds like you both want a good outcome, but have different strategies," "Perhaps we could try combining both ideas," "So, if I understand correctly, Juan, you think... and Maria, you propose... Is that right?" The teacher also models the pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /gr/ in "growth" and /kr/ in "create," and stress patterns in "PROgress" (noun) vs. "proGRESS" (verb), focusing on vocabulary related to academic success and problem-solving (e.g., strategy, progress, solution, focus).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When you mediate, are you taking one person's side, or helping everyone agree?" (Expected answer: Helping everyone agree.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'Let's brainstorm some solutions,' am I asking you to argue or to think of new ideas?" (Expected answer: To think of new ideas.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording or transcript of a short dialogue (2-3 minutes) between two students planning their "Academic Advice Podcast." The dialogue includes one student explaining an academic article about "effective study strategies" (summarizing) and another student having doubts about how to present the advice, leading to a mild disagreement which is then mediated by a third student using target phrases. The dialogue explicitly uses the second conditional for giving advice and modals (should, must, can).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend how to summarize information from an academic text and how to use language to mediate disagreements effectively in a group setting. They will identify expressions for suggesting solutions and ensuring all opinions are heard, focusing on pragmatic functions of asking for and giving advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main challenge the students are facing with their podcast project?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The academic article suggests that setting study goals improves focus." "The mediator immediately chose one student's idea over the other's."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match the problem (e.g., "difficulty summarizing") with the solution suggested by the mediator (e.g., "break down the article into key points").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using second conditional for giving hypothetical advice ("If you studied harder, you would get better grades."), modals for direct advice ("You should complete assignments on time."), and future simple for first conditional ("If you focus on your studies, you will achieve your goals."). Focus on vocabulary: achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, solution, strategy, progress, facilitate, encourage, suggest, discuss.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> Students complete sentences that require mediation or advice using the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "If I were more _________ (organize/organized), I _________ (achieve) my academic goals," "To resolve this conflict, we _________ (should/must) find a fair _________ (solution)." "The article _________ (explain) that setting _________ (study goals) _________ (improve) focus."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Rephrasing for Mediation):</b> Students work in small groups. The teacher provides two contrasting opinions related to academic tasks (e.g., "Student A thinks group projects are useless because one person always does all the work. Student B believes group projects are essential for developing teamwork skills."). Students must rephrase both opinions neutrally and suggest a compromise using mediating language (e.g., "It seems A values individual effort, while B values collaborative skill development. Perhaps if we set clear roles and responsibilities, everyone will contribute."). They practice stress patterns for words like 'progress' (noun/verb) if they use it in their rephrasing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Summarizing Advice):</b> Students are given short paragraphs (2-3 sentences each) summarizing different academic advice articles (e.g., about time management, active listening, or reflective practice). In pairs, they take turns summarizing their paragraph's main point to their partner, as if for the "Academic Advice Podcast," and then their partner asks clarifying questions or offers further advice using the second conditional. (e.g., "So, if I understand, the main strategy is...? If I used that strategy, I would...?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Academic Achievement and Skill Development."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Podcast Planning Mediation Session." Students work in their "Academic Advice Podcast" groups. The task involves mediating the final planning stages of their podcast, focusing on summarizing key advice for 9th graders and resolving any disagreements on content or presentation style. Each group is given a specific "challenge" (e.g., "Two members disagree on whether to include advice about choosing a learning style or focusing on discipline," or "One member thinks the advice is too complex for 9th graders, another thinks it's too simple").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive their group's specific challenge related to the "Academic Advice Podcast" project.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One student in each group acts as a designated mediator (or roles can rotate).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mediator facilitates the discussion, ensuring everyone's opinions are heard by rephrasing ideas (Sociolinguistic: "Mariana suggested that if we combine both ideas, we can create a stronger project. What does everyone think?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group must summarize the core advice points they intend to give in their podcast episode, ensuring clarity and target-audience appropriateness, using modals for advice (Pragmatic: "You should complete your assignments on time to succeed academically.") and second conditional (Grammar: "If you studied in a quiet place, you would concentrate better.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The group collaboratively decides on a solution for their challenge, explaining their reasoning and the resulting podcast plan.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their resolved conflict and their summarized advice for the "Academic Advice Podcast" to the class (1-2 minutes per group). They explain how they mediated the disagreement and the final strategy for their podcast content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation, especially of target vocabulary and stress patterns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Effectively uses words like achievement, strategy, solution, facilitate, encourage, focus, discipline.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion (Mediation):</b> Rephrases ideas clearly, suggests fair solutions, ensures all opinions are heard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion (Summarization):</b> Summarizes main points of academic advice effectively for the target audience (9th graders).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Interaction:</b> Actively participates in group mediation, uses inclusive language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of second conditional, modals for advice, and first conditional.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one phrase you used today to help mediate a disagreement in your group."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the most important piece of advice your group plans to share in the podcast, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing mediation skills today help your group develop your Academic Advice Podcast?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific mediation strategy or advice did you find most useful, and how could you apply it to future group projects?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting mediation skills for the podcast project? Students actively engaged in rephrasing and proposing solutions. The specific challenges for each group encouraged authentic mediation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? (Note: This is the last lesson of the sequence. For future iterations or a follow-up, consider providing more examples of mediating complex academic discussions or introducing nuanced language for persuasion within mediation. Perhaps a wider range of pronunciation challenges for B1.2.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence: This lesson successfully integrated all previous skills into the final project, allowing students to apply vocabulary, grammar, reading, writing, listening, and speaking skills within a mediation context to produce a relevant 21st-century artifact (the podcast). This concludes the theme "Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?". The next theme will build on these collaborative and communicative skills in a new context.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>